#[ crates/ directory layout ]

This directory is flat. Grouping is expressed through the crate name's
prefix family, not through subdirectories -- see
each crate's `//!` module doc for what it actually does and
how they depend on each other; this file is just the naming map.

A family is spelled `tomet-<family>` for the crate that *is* the family,
and `tomet-<family>-<name>` for the rest. Three families have a bare
centre (`semantics`, `format`, `workspace`) and two do not (`syntax`,
`convert`), which is why the root `.writ.tmt`'s `@layers` patterns are
`tomet-semantics*` in the first case and `tomet-convert-*` in the second
-- the hyphen is present exactly when there is no bare centre to match.

@dir(crates/tomet-format) was `crates/tomet-formatter` until 2026-09-06, the
one crate that did not follow this. The table below listed it as a member
of `tomet-format-*` while the directory said otherwise, and `@layers` had
been loosened to `tomet-format*` to cope -- a pattern that read like the
`tomet-semantics*` case without having its reason. Renaming the directory
made the loosening correct instead of coincidental. The package is still
`tomet-formatter`; only the directory moved.

Each package's Cargo.toml `name` is unprefixed by family (e.g.
`tomet-ast`, unchanged since before this naming scheme) even though
its directory carries the family prefix (`tomet-syntax-ast`) --
directory name and package name are allowed to differ, and this is
intentional: `use tomet_ast::...` and every dependency reference
stays exactly as short as it always was. Only the directory name (and
therefore path-lookup) carries the family grouping.

@codeblock(lang:text)+++
tomet-syntax-*      grammar/parsing: ast, cst, lexer, parser, tree.
                         Turns source text into CST/AST and provides pure tree schemas.

tomet-semantics*     semantics (bare, no suffix -- the crate IS the category),
                         semantics-validator, semantics-compute, semantics-resolver.
                         What a parsed Element means / whether it's valid, runtime ${...} expression eval, and reference linking.

tomet-transform      pure in-memory AST refactoring & macro rewrite rules.

tomet-convert-*      html, markdown, pandoc, typst.
                         Conversion to/from *external* document formats.

tomet-format*        format (bare, no suffix -- publishes tomet-formatter),
                         format-field-utils, format-printer, format-style.
                         .tmt source text production, formatting, and styling.

tomet-workspace*     workspace (bare, no suffix),
                         workspace-indexer, workspace-links.
                         Multi-file batch I/O, directory scanning, and link checking.
                         Config discovery is tomet-config, which sits below these.

serde_tomet,          external-ecosystem adapters. Follow their
tree-sitter-tomet     host ecosystem's own naming convention
                         (serde_*, tree-sitter-*) rather than
                         tomet's -- that's what makes them
                         discoverable to serde/tree-sitter tooling.
+++

`tomet-tui` lives in @dir(apps/tui), not here -- it's consumed as a
subcommand of @dir(apps/cli), not depended on by any other crate, so it's
an application rather than a library.

Moving a crate only requires updating its path in the root
`Cargo.toml` (`members` list and `[workspace.dependencies]` `path =`
value) -- package names and `{ workspace = true }` dependency keys
never change, so no `use` statement anywhere needs touching. Watch out
for `include_str!`/`include_bytes!` literals using `../`-relative paths
reaching outside the crate's own directory, and for hardcoded
`.parent().unwrap()` chains walking up to the repo root -- both are
sensitive to how many directories deep the crate sits, which a rename
alone doesn't change, but a nesting depth change would.

No crate in here does either of those anymore. The tests that used to
(reaching up for the shared `.tmt` corpus) live in the @dir(tests/) package
(`tomet-tests`) now, which owns that corpus outright -- see
@file(tests/README.md). Keep it that way: a test that needs the shared corpus
belongs there, not in a crate reaching out of its own directory for it.
